  "text": ".batch..LayoutInflater introduction to the problem\nתפריט פה!\n\n.WriteHeader\n\n扅Start with the key formula for a regular pentagon.\n\n僚By drawing a diagonal AE, we can integrate the properties of a regular pentagon with circles and trigonometric ratios.\n\n僚angle FAE is 30 degrees, and diagonal AE bisects the angle into two 30 degree angles.\n\n僚To find the length of segment EF, we need to use the radius of the circle circumscribed about the pentagon.\n\n僚For a regular pentagon with side length $s$, the radius $R$ of the circumscribed circle is equal to $\\frac{s}{2\\sin(36\\degree)}$.\n\n僚Draw a perpendicular from point E to side AB and call the intersection point P; from P draw a perpendicular to diagonal AE and let the intersection point be Q. Now QF = PQ.\n\n僚Using the circle theorem again, $\\angle EQR=\\frac{2\\pi}{5}$, $\\angle FQR=\\frac{2\\pi}{5}-\\frac{\\pi}{4}= \\frac{3\\pi}{20}$, then by circle theorem $QR=R$. It also follows from $\\angle ERQ=\\frac{\\pi}{5}$ that $ER=R\\cos(\\frac{\\pi}{3})=\\frac{R}{2}$.\n\n僚From $\\angle EQF=\\frac{3\\pi}{20}$, it follows that $\\tan(\\angle EQF)=\\frac{QR}{FQ}=\\frac{r}{q}$. Finally, since $\\angle FQPA=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$, it follows that $\\tan(\\frac{3\\pi}{20})=\\frac{s}{f}$, which we know from this diagram can be simplified to $\\frac{5-\\sqrt{5}}{4}=\\frac{s}{qu}$. Eliminating $q$ and substituting $q$ in terms of $s$ then yields $EF=ER+FQ=\\frac{ R}{2}+\\frac{s}{qu} R= \\frac{s^2}{2(5-\\sqrt{5})}$.\n\n僚ATFORMATEX:\n\n.WRITEBEGINmathmode\nThus, $EF=s{\\sqrt{5-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}}/2}\\sim 0.76a$.\n.WRITEENDmathmode\n\n.WriteASBEGINmathChainedOrderingSet{ACSIIpackage New.math}; #mathcustomtype number{size=false};\nbitrary\n WriteASBlockMathEndBlock EndASEND.瀹བ)\r\n\r\nWriteMathChained",
